<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06SR">Example <span data-tag="06SR">4.8.4.2</span>.</a> Suppose that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ are the nerves of categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$, so that $F$ is obtained from a functor $F_0: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$. In this case, Theorem <a data-tag="01JX" href="/tag/01JX">4.8.4.1</a> reduces to the assertion that $F_0$ is an equivalence of categories if and only if it is essentially surjective, full, and faithful. See Example <a data-tag="06S5" href="/tag/06S5">4.8.2.8</a>. </p>
